1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
The Sears Tower, the World Trade Center,

2
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:13,540
the Empire State Building, the tallest buildings in the world.

3
00:00:13,640 --> 00:00:16,480
What sends them soaring so high?

4
00:00:16,580 --> 00:00:21,060
A heady mixture of glass and steel, high wire act and ego,

5
00:00:21,160 --> 00:00:25,700
a dash of manifest destiny, and the lure of money,

6
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:27,220
lots of money.

7
00:00:27,221 --> 00:00:32,220
Now, Skyscrapers, a race toward the heavens on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:57,220 --> 00:00:59,760
The defining image of urban America

9
00:00:59,860 --> 00:01:05,160
is the city skyline, a team of big-shouldered friends.

10
00:01:05,260 --> 00:01:08,560
They're pushy, at times inspired,

11
00:01:08,660 --> 00:01:13,800
and there's never been anything like them, ever.

12
00:01:13,900 --> 00:01:18,380
The skyscraper is and was born in America.

13
00:01:18,480 --> 00:01:20,340
It's grown up in America.

14
00:01:20,440 --> 00:01:23,220
And in many respects, it's really a symbol

15
00:01:23,320 --> 00:01:26,420
of an American century and the triumph

16
00:01:26,421 --> 00:01:31,820
of capitalism and really of American values.

17
00:01:31,920 --> 00:01:34,920
A skyscraper is a tall, commercial building

18
00:01:35,020 --> 00:01:38,860
with an internal steel skeleton support system.

19
00:01:38,960 --> 00:01:40,960
The external walls of a skyscraper

20
00:01:41,060 --> 00:01:43,680
do not support its structure.

21
00:01:43,780 --> 00:01:47,540
But not all tall buildings are skyscrapers.

22
00:01:47,640 --> 00:01:50,820
Towers are also tall buildings made of steel,

23
00:01:50,920 --> 00:01:53,720
but towers are not built to provide places for people

24
00:01:53,820 --> 00:01:55,320
to live and work.

25
00:01:55,321 --> 00:01:57,820
The concept of constructing tall buildings

26
00:01:57,920 --> 00:01:59,560
is not a new one.

27
00:01:59,660 --> 00:02:03,460
You know, the Bible has the story of the Tower of Babel,

28
00:02:03,560 --> 00:02:09,060
an example of human pride that takes us too far.

29
00:02:09,160 --> 00:02:09,860
What's the phrase?

30
00:02:09,861 --> 00:02:12,080
Pride goeth before the fall.

31
00:02:12,180 --> 00:02:16,180
Human beings have always tried to build as tall as we could.

32
00:02:16,280 --> 00:02:18,180
Earlier civilizations were often

33
00:02:18,280 --> 00:02:22,480
motivated by three factors when they constructed tall buildings.

34
00:02:22,481 --> 00:02:25,720
One was a desire to please the gods.

35
00:02:25,820 --> 00:02:30,120
This inspired the ancient pyramids of Egypt.

36
00:02:30,220 --> 00:02:32,820
Another was a lack of space, which often

37
00:02:32,920 --> 00:02:36,800
forced people to build upward, hence the cliff dwellings

38
00:02:36,900 --> 00:02:39,840
of the American Southwest.

39
00:02:39,940 --> 00:02:42,900
A lack of space in urban areas prompted owners

40
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:46,100
in medieval Europe to add additional stories to already

41
00:02:46,200 --> 00:02:47,980
completed offices and shops.

42
00:02:47,981 --> 00:02:51,920
A third factor was ego.

43
00:02:52,020 --> 00:02:55,220
The effort was not so much to get closer to God

44
00:02:55,320 --> 00:02:58,720
but to be equal to him.

45
00:02:58,820 --> 00:03:00,800
Well, look at the Gothic cathedrals.

46
00:03:00,900 --> 00:03:03,000
It was a battle between the bishops.

47
00:03:03,100 --> 00:03:07,640
My name is 130 feet high instead of 100 feet high

48
00:03:07,740 --> 00:03:09,340
and things like that.

49
00:03:09,440 --> 00:03:12,380
But it was more than just a battle between the bishops.

50
00:03:12,480 --> 00:03:14,380
The cathedrals of Europe were often

51
00:03:14,480 --> 00:03:17,440
the product of collective ego that

52
00:03:17,441 --> 00:03:21,080
that translated as civic pride.

53
00:03:21,180 --> 00:03:25,580
At heights approaching 500 feet, these towering stone structures

54
00:03:25,680 --> 00:03:30,280
had flying buttresses to support their massive thick walls.

55
00:03:30,380 --> 00:03:33,920
The Eiffel Tower was partly the product of civic pride that

56
00:03:34,020 --> 00:03:36,920
swept France in the late 19th century.

57
00:03:37,020 --> 00:03:39,860
Built for the 1889 World's Exposition,

58
00:03:39,960 --> 00:03:42,760
it served no practical purpose.

59
00:03:42,860 --> 00:03:45,660
It was, however, made of iron.

60
00:03:45,661 --> 00:03:47,660
The use of iron allowed for the tower

61
00:03:47,760 --> 00:03:51,100
to reach a height of 986 feet.

62
00:03:51,200 --> 00:03:53,900
But office buildings in the mid-19th century

63
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:57,240
continued to use ancient construction methods.

64
00:03:57,340 --> 00:04:00,080
When architects first designed tall buildings,

65
00:04:00,180 --> 00:04:03,580
the only way that they could figure out how to do it

66
00:04:03,680 --> 00:04:09,120
was to use the masonry itself to support the upper floors

67
00:04:09,220 --> 00:04:10,220
of the building.

68
00:04:10,320 --> 00:04:14,620
So that when you build a very tall building

69
00:04:14,621 --> 00:04:19,060
that was a masonry structure, you ended up with walls

70
00:04:19,160 --> 00:04:22,020
that were maybe nine feet thick.

71
00:04:22,120 --> 00:04:25,560
The taller the buildings, the thicker the walls,

72
00:04:25,660 --> 00:04:28,560
reducing valuable rental space.

73
00:04:28,660 --> 00:04:31,240
When wrought iron was introduced in the mid-1800s,

74
00:04:31,340 --> 00:04:34,500
wall thickness decreased.

75
00:04:34,600 --> 00:04:37,280
However, wrought iron had inherent problems,

76
00:04:37,380 --> 00:04:39,440
and buildings using wrought iron supports

77
00:04:39,540 --> 00:04:42,420
never reached great heights.

78
00:04:42,421 --> 00:04:45,860
In the late 1800s, architects began using a far stronger

79
00:04:45,960 --> 00:04:47,500
material.

80
00:04:47,600 --> 00:04:51,860
It would give America the height advantage.

81
00:04:51,960 --> 00:04:56,200
Henry Bessemer introduced a way of converting large amounts

82
00:04:56,300 --> 00:04:59,980
of iron to steel.

83
00:05:00,080 --> 00:05:02,800
By forcing air through molten iron,

84
00:05:02,900 --> 00:05:04,780
he purged impurities from the metal

85
00:05:04,880 --> 00:05:09,440
and produced the significantly stronger steel.

86
00:05:09,441 --> 00:05:13,620
Steel began to come in in the 1880s,

87
00:05:13,720 --> 00:05:16,740
largely as a result of the improvements in the Bessemer

88
00:05:16,840 --> 00:05:20,680
process of strengthening steel.

89
00:05:20,780 --> 00:05:24,960
Cast iron is a less efficient material for carrying loads.

90
00:05:25,060 --> 00:05:28,020
Steel is tensile strong.

91
00:05:28,120 --> 00:05:31,200
It's very good in a bending situation.

92
00:05:31,300 --> 00:05:35,460
Cast iron is brittle, and it'll break if you try to bend it.

93
00:05:35,560 --> 00:05:38,260
America began the mass production of steel

94
00:05:38,261 --> 00:05:40,060
in the late 1800s.

95
00:05:40,160 --> 00:05:42,600
But it wasn't until a new development in the way

96
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:46,200
steel beams were put together that steel became

97
00:05:46,300 --> 00:05:51,400
the essential ingredient for building skyscrapers.

98
00:05:51,500 --> 00:05:55,740
In 1848, James Bogardus introduced a revolutionary

99
00:05:55,840 --> 00:05:57,540
construction method.

100
00:05:57,640 --> 00:06:01,380
His design used interconnected wrought iron beams

101
00:06:01,480 --> 00:06:05,580
to support a building from within.

102
00:06:05,581 --> 00:06:08,780
When you talk about skyscrapers,

103
00:06:08,880 --> 00:06:11,180
you have to remember that the walls

104
00:06:11,280 --> 00:06:13,220
are not doing anything to support the building.

105
00:06:13,320 --> 00:06:16,560
A skyscraper is analogous to the human frame.

106
00:06:16,660 --> 00:06:20,200
A skyscraper is a skeleton of steel

107
00:06:20,300 --> 00:06:22,300
supporting the building, just as your body

108
00:06:22,400 --> 00:06:25,700
is a skeleton of bone supporting yours.

109
00:06:25,800 --> 00:06:28,440
This new skeletal or cage construction

110
00:06:28,540 --> 00:06:30,600
offered many benefits.

111
00:06:30,700 --> 00:06:33,500
The load of the building was distributed equally

112
00:06:33,501 --> 00:06:37,100
over the entire internal framework, instead of resting

113
00:06:37,200 --> 00:06:39,640
on the exterior walls.

114
00:06:39,740 --> 00:06:42,940
This allowed for taller structures with larger windows

115
00:06:43,040 --> 00:06:47,540
in an era before electric lights and air conditioning.

116
00:06:47,640 --> 00:06:49,620
But another drawback to tall buildings

117
00:06:49,720 --> 00:06:52,260
was the height of the building itself.

118
00:06:52,360 --> 00:06:55,620
Why build a nine-story building if no one was willing to climb

119
00:06:55,720 --> 00:06:58,760
nine flights of stairs?

120
00:06:58,860 --> 00:07:03,500
Elijah Graves Otis would remove this last great obstacle.

121
00:07:03,501 --> 00:07:06,100
At the New York Exposition of 1853,

122
00:07:06,200 --> 00:07:08,680
Otis introduced a revolutionary means

123
00:07:08,780 --> 00:07:12,040
of vertical transportation.

124
00:07:12,140 --> 00:07:15,240
The device moved goods and people from one floor

125
00:07:15,340 --> 00:07:17,080
to the next.

126
00:07:17,180 --> 00:07:19,980
A box connected by cables to a counterweight

127
00:07:20,080 --> 00:07:24,060
floated easily between floors.

128
00:07:24,160 --> 00:07:27,420
In 1857, the Otis Elevator Company

129
00:07:27,520 --> 00:07:31,600
installed the very first steam-powered passenger elevator

130
00:07:31,601 --> 00:07:32,860
in a New York building.

131
00:07:35,900 --> 00:07:38,800
America had collected all of the raw materials

132
00:07:38,900 --> 00:07:41,240
for skyscraper construction.

133
00:07:41,340 --> 00:07:43,840
The final ingredient, the one that would send skyscrapers

134
00:07:43,940 --> 00:07:47,920
soaring higher and higher, was profit.

135
00:07:48,020 --> 00:07:52,580
As architect Cass Gilbert said, a skyscraper is a machine

136
00:07:52,680 --> 00:07:55,220
that makes the land pay.

137
00:07:55,320 --> 00:07:58,320
For many years, historians of Chicago architecture

138
00:07:58,420 --> 00:08:01,360
felt that the home insurance building designed by William

139
00:08:01,361 --> 00:08:07,460
William LeBaron Jenny from 1884 was the moment, the moment,

140
00:08:07,461 --> 00:08:11,980
when all of the right technical innovations came together

141
00:08:11,981 --> 00:08:15,100
to result in a skyscraper.

142
00:08:15,101 --> 00:08:17,840
He was trying so hard to get it all together.

143
00:08:17,841 --> 00:08:23,480
And it was his innovations that led to the full blown skyscrapers

144
00:08:23,481 --> 00:08:26,220
of the 1890s.

145
00:08:26,221 --> 00:08:29,920
Jenny's building was only 10 stories tall.

146
00:08:29,921 --> 00:08:33,960
But the lack of height did not diminish his contribution.

147
00:08:33,961 --> 00:08:36,600
Jenny combined the essential elements that would,

148
00:08:36,601 --> 00:08:40,840
in just a few short years, allow for the tallest buildings

149
00:08:40,841 --> 00:08:42,500
in the world.

150
00:08:42,501 --> 00:08:46,460
It would happen in an environment of economic explosion,

151
00:08:46,461 --> 00:08:50,080
corporate domination, and inflated personal ego.

152
00:08:50,081 --> 00:09:00,020
America in the 1880s was undergoing a transformation,

153
00:09:00,120 --> 00:09:06,460
particularly in her two largest cities, New York and Chicago.

154
00:09:06,560 --> 00:09:10,560
They were competing to become the economic and social capital

155
00:09:10,660 --> 00:09:13,200
of the country.

156
00:09:13,300 --> 00:09:16,580
A strong economy pushed the need for new factories

157
00:09:16,581 --> 00:09:18,480
factories and offices.

158
00:09:18,580 --> 00:09:21,920
But the scarcity of land was a problem.

159
00:09:22,020 --> 00:09:24,320
The solution was the skyscraper.

160
00:09:27,420 --> 00:09:30,220
High rise design at this time reflected the importance

161
00:09:30,320 --> 00:09:32,860
of natural light and airflow.

162
00:09:32,960 --> 00:09:35,960
Offices were constructed to be closer to windows

163
00:09:36,060 --> 00:09:39,240
for both lighting and ventilation.

164
00:09:39,340 --> 00:09:43,280
From the first tall buildings in the 1880s and 1890s

165
00:09:43,281 --> 00:09:47,580
through the 1940s, all buildings depended on nature

166
00:09:47,680 --> 00:09:51,060
and took their shape dependent on the forces of nature.

167
00:09:51,160 --> 00:09:54,020
I always remember the story about the Reliance building

168
00:09:54,120 --> 00:09:55,960
where there were a lot of dentists at work.

169
00:09:56,060 --> 00:09:58,720
So the Reliance has these huge windows,

170
00:09:58,820 --> 00:10:00,860
like 10 feet tall or something.

171
00:10:00,960 --> 00:10:02,760
And the dentists were at work, and they

172
00:10:02,860 --> 00:10:04,600
needed to see into someone's mouth.

173
00:10:04,700 --> 00:10:06,900
There was a real functional reason for these buildings

174
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:09,000
to have these enormous expanses of glass.

175
00:10:09,100 --> 00:10:10,900
It was really that simple.

176
00:10:10,901 --> 00:10:14,200
Skyscrapers were growing taller, but still

177
00:10:14,300 --> 00:10:18,300
resembled the old style of masonry buildings.

178
00:10:18,400 --> 00:10:20,540
The person who proposed giving tall buildings

179
00:10:20,640 --> 00:10:25,320
their own characteristic look was the Chicago architect,

180
00:10:25,420 --> 00:10:28,380
Louis Sullivan.

181
00:10:28,480 --> 00:10:32,180
Louis Sullivan is acknowledged as the father

182
00:10:32,280 --> 00:10:36,500
of American architecture because he espoused the fact

183
00:10:36,600 --> 00:10:39,800
that there should be an architecture that

184
00:10:39,801 --> 00:10:42,000
that is unique to America.

185
00:10:42,100 --> 00:10:44,140
He tried to design the skyscraper

186
00:10:44,240 --> 00:10:46,900
so that it actually expressed its height.

187
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:49,740
And his philosophy was that a skyscraper should have

188
00:10:49,840 --> 00:10:51,180
three distinct parts.

189
00:10:51,280 --> 00:10:54,880
It should have a base, a very solid base.

190
00:10:54,980 --> 00:10:57,020
It should have a long shaft.

191
00:10:57,120 --> 00:10:59,680
And it should have something to end the building,

192
00:10:59,780 --> 00:11:01,620
like a cornice.

193
00:11:01,720 --> 00:11:05,620
Chicago buildings such as Sullivan's 275-foot-high

194
00:11:05,720 --> 00:11:08,600
auditorium building built in 1889

195
00:11:08,601 --> 00:11:12,300
demonstrated this bold assertion of height.

196
00:11:12,400 --> 00:11:15,540
Built partially to rival New York's Metropolitan Opera

197
00:11:15,640 --> 00:11:17,680
House, the auditorium building came

198
00:11:17,780 --> 00:11:20,440
to symbolize the cultural significance Chicago

199
00:11:20,540 --> 00:11:22,180
was quickly gaining.

200
00:11:22,280 --> 00:11:25,120
It also held the title of Chicago's tallest building

201
00:11:25,220 --> 00:11:27,220
at 22 stories.

202
00:11:27,320 --> 00:11:29,760
There always was a keen sense of rivalry

203
00:11:29,860 --> 00:11:31,360
between Chicago and New York.

204
00:11:31,460 --> 00:11:33,560
And one way that this was played out

205
00:11:33,660 --> 00:11:38,100
was with the title of world's tallest building.

206
00:11:38,101 --> 00:11:40,140
Burgeoning construction in both cities

207
00:11:40,240 --> 00:11:44,100
forced more and more buildings to greater heights.

208
00:11:44,200 --> 00:11:47,540
It was difficult keeping track of the dizzying succession

209
00:11:47,640 --> 00:11:49,680
of tallest buildings.

210
00:11:49,780 --> 00:11:55,120
But in 1890, one building stood out and stood up decisively

211
00:11:55,220 --> 00:11:56,320
to claim the prize.

212
00:12:02,720 --> 00:12:04,820
When newspaperman Joseph Pulitzer

213
00:12:04,821 --> 00:12:08,360
took over the New York World newspaper in 1883,

214
00:12:08,460 --> 00:12:11,020
circulation exploded.

215
00:12:11,120 --> 00:12:13,960
Lack of space necessitated a new office structure

216
00:12:14,060 --> 00:12:18,400
for the paper, but ego pushed it to the altitudinal boundary

217
00:12:18,500 --> 00:12:21,340
no office building had ever broken.

218
00:12:21,440 --> 00:12:24,640
Built to eclipse the Sun Building, his competitor's paper

219
00:12:24,740 --> 00:12:28,540
across the street, the 18-story New York World Building

220
00:12:28,640 --> 00:12:31,720
reached an astounding 309 feet.

221
00:12:31,721 --> 00:12:34,220
It was the world's tallest building for two years.

222
00:12:37,220 --> 00:12:39,260
The title was wrested back from New York

223
00:12:39,360 --> 00:12:43,400
and held for a brief time by the city on the prairie.

224
00:12:43,500 --> 00:12:47,400
In 1892, Chicago's 22-story Masonic Temple

225
00:12:47,500 --> 00:12:50,480
became the tallest building in the world.

226
00:12:50,580 --> 00:12:52,580
The birthplace of the skyscraper had

227
00:12:52,680 --> 00:12:55,480
reclaimed the title by just a few feet.

228
00:12:55,481 --> 00:13:00,480
But in 1893, Chicago initiated one of the nation's first limits

229
00:13:00,580 --> 00:13:02,480
on building height.

230
00:13:02,580 --> 00:13:05,960
Protested by developers who feared dwindling profits,

231
00:13:06,060 --> 00:13:11,000
the new regulation restricted buildings to 130 feet.

232
00:13:11,100 --> 00:13:14,260
They limited the height because of real estate pressures,

233
00:13:14,360 --> 00:13:15,700
really.

234
00:13:15,800 --> 00:13:17,740
So in Chicago, there were no buildings

235
00:13:17,840 --> 00:13:21,380
that were taller than about 11 stories after 1892.

236
00:13:21,480 --> 00:13:24,680
Meanwhile, in New York, the city was

237
00:13:24,681 --> 00:13:27,140
Meanwhile, in New York, buildings kept growing

238
00:13:27,240 --> 00:13:29,060
taller and taller.

239
00:13:29,160 --> 00:13:30,980
For the next 90 years, the race

240
00:13:31,080 --> 00:13:33,160
to create the world's tallest building

241
00:13:33,260 --> 00:13:37,100
would take place in New York City.

242
00:13:37,200 --> 00:13:39,800
Manhattan is hemmed in by water.

243
00:13:39,900 --> 00:13:43,800
So since you can't expand horizontally,

244
00:13:43,900 --> 00:13:45,440
you expand vertically.

245
00:13:45,540 --> 00:13:47,700
Buildings started going up that were tall.

246
00:13:47,800 --> 00:13:50,880
Then buildings started going up that were taller still.

247
00:13:50,980 --> 00:13:53,540
The more tenants a building could sustain,

248
00:13:53,541 --> 00:13:54,780
the profit.

249
00:13:54,880 --> 00:13:57,240
But tenants were picky in a time when office space

250
00:13:57,340 --> 00:13:58,480
was plentiful.

251
00:13:58,580 --> 00:14:02,280
Offices with windows demanded the highest rents.

252
00:14:02,380 --> 00:14:04,980
Buildings were designed with this in mind,

253
00:14:05,080 --> 00:14:08,160
which created similar-looking buildings with large windows

254
00:14:08,260 --> 00:14:11,800
and shallow office spaces.

255
00:14:11,900 --> 00:14:13,600
As architects and engineers learned

256
00:14:13,700 --> 00:14:16,660
how to send skyscrapers higher and higher,

257
00:14:16,760 --> 00:14:22,100
designers had to ask, how high should a building be?

258
00:14:22,101 --> 00:14:25,000
From an economic standpoint, a building could actually

259
00:14:25,100 --> 00:14:26,600
be too tall.

260
00:14:26,700 --> 00:14:29,780
The cost of construction increased exponentially

261
00:14:29,880 --> 00:14:31,340
as height grew.

262
00:14:31,440 --> 00:14:35,020
For most buildings, it was a simple equation of cost

263
00:14:35,120 --> 00:14:38,920
versus estimated return.

264
00:14:39,020 --> 00:14:41,160
But in the race to be the tallest,

265
00:14:41,260 --> 00:14:45,360
several buildings owed almost as much to ego as they

266
00:14:45,460 --> 00:14:47,920
did to economics.

267
00:14:48,020 --> 00:14:51,620
The competition for the really spectacular tall

268
00:14:51,621 --> 00:14:55,620
Towers really begins in about 1906,

269
00:14:55,720 --> 00:14:58,420
1907 when the Singer Building plans their tower

270
00:14:58,520 --> 00:15:00,120
on Lower Broadway in New York.

271
00:15:00,220 --> 00:15:02,900
And that is pretty much the pattern in New York

272
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:05,940
with the succession of tallest towers.

273
00:15:06,040 --> 00:15:09,840
In 1906, the Singer Company began construction

274
00:15:09,940 --> 00:15:12,880
on what would be the tallest office building in the world.

275
00:15:12,980 --> 00:15:16,140
And it would be the tallest not by a few stories,

276
00:15:16,240 --> 00:15:19,380
but by 200 feet.

277
00:15:19,381 --> 00:15:23,280
Structural design required special attention.

278
00:15:23,380 --> 00:15:25,860
An innovative cross-bracing system

279
00:15:25,960 --> 00:15:29,560
counteracted extreme wind forces on the tower.

280
00:15:29,660 --> 00:15:33,800
Completed in under one year, the 41-story Singer building

281
00:15:33,900 --> 00:15:37,540
reached 612 feet with an observation deck

282
00:15:37,640 --> 00:15:40,500
where for a few cents a person could have the best view

283
00:15:40,600 --> 00:15:41,900
the city had to offer.

284
00:15:44,880 --> 00:15:46,940
Only three years after the Singer Tower,

285
00:15:46,941 --> 00:15:50,280
the Metropolitan Life Insurance Company completed work

286
00:15:50,380 --> 00:15:53,820
on a giant 50-story building.

287
00:15:53,920 --> 00:15:57,820
In 1909, it beat the Singer Building by 88 feet,

288
00:15:57,920 --> 00:16:00,120
becoming the world's tallest building,

289
00:16:00,220 --> 00:16:04,360
though it only wore the crown for a year.

290
00:16:04,460 --> 00:16:07,460
It was at this moment that Frank W. Woolworth announced

291
00:16:07,560 --> 00:16:12,440
plans to build the world's most spectacular skyscraper.

292
00:16:12,540 --> 00:16:15,440
Woolworth had an empire of five and dime stores

293
00:16:15,441 --> 00:16:17,880
and wanted to make a statement.

294
00:16:17,980 --> 00:16:21,380
He decided to build not only the tallest skyscraper in the world,

295
00:16:21,480 --> 00:16:25,580
but quite possibly the most elaborate.

296
00:16:25,680 --> 00:16:29,760
Following Sullivan's idea of proper skyscraper aesthetics,

297
00:16:29,860 --> 00:16:34,000
the building would have a heavy base, long shaft,

298
00:16:34,100 --> 00:16:35,700
and ornate cap.

299
00:16:35,800 --> 00:16:38,000
Woolworth and his architect Cass Gilbert

300
00:16:38,100 --> 00:16:42,300
chose a highly ornate Gothic theme.

301
00:16:42,400 --> 00:16:44,040
Looking at Woolworth, you would never

302
00:16:44,041 --> 00:16:47,780
guess that it had its origins in humble five and dime

303
00:16:47,880 --> 00:16:52,180
stores with its Gothic curlicues and design details,

304
00:16:52,280 --> 00:16:54,680
which kind of give the impression that it's

305
00:16:54,780 --> 00:16:56,280
almost from the medieval era.

306
00:16:56,380 --> 00:16:58,180
In fact, Woolworth earned the nickname

307
00:16:58,280 --> 00:17:00,800
the Cathedral of Commerce.

308
00:17:00,900 --> 00:17:03,320
The exterior would be built of terracotta

309
00:17:03,420 --> 00:17:07,060
and rise some 55 stories, besting the MetLife

310
00:17:07,160 --> 00:17:09,800
building by 92 feet.

311
00:17:09,900 --> 00:17:12,800
Unlike previous short-time title holders,

312
00:17:12,801 --> 00:17:15,360
Woolworth's building would be world's tallest

313
00:17:15,460 --> 00:17:20,240
for some 16 years.

314
00:17:20,340 --> 00:17:25,320
On the night of April 24, 1913, President Woodrow Wilson

315
00:17:25,420 --> 00:17:28,180
from the White House threw a switch that lit up

316
00:17:28,280 --> 00:17:31,080
the enormous edifice.

317
00:17:31,180 --> 00:17:34,080
Colored and white lights bathe the terracotta walls

318
00:17:34,180 --> 00:17:36,620
in a glorious presentation.

319
00:17:36,720 --> 00:17:41,120
Never before had a building gained such attention.

320
00:17:41,121 --> 00:17:44,220
Borrowing a style once reserved for houses of God,

321
00:17:44,320 --> 00:17:47,660
the Gothic Woolworth building reflected changing values

322
00:17:47,760 --> 00:17:50,360
in capitalistic America.

323
00:17:50,460 --> 00:17:54,400
Its completion, plus other unrestricted construction,

324
00:17:54,500 --> 00:17:56,440
also triggered a deepening concern

325
00:17:56,540 --> 00:17:59,040
about the negative effects of overbuilding.

326
00:17:59,140 --> 00:18:02,900
You can walk down Wall Street and not see the sun

327
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:04,480
at high noon.

328
00:18:04,580 --> 00:18:08,240
So it wasn't just the municipal government.

329
00:18:08,340 --> 00:18:09,340
It was planners.

330
00:18:09,440 --> 00:18:10,580
It was architects.

331
00:18:10,581 --> 00:18:12,080
who said, we have to stop this.

332
00:18:12,180 --> 00:18:13,020
This is crazy.

333
00:18:13,120 --> 00:18:16,320
We're overbuilding, and we're killing the city.

334
00:18:16,420 --> 00:18:18,420
Fueling the demand for regulation

335
00:18:18,520 --> 00:18:22,120
was the new equitable building, a gargantuan structure

336
00:18:22,220 --> 00:18:24,620
completed in 1916.

337
00:18:24,720 --> 00:18:28,340
People vehemently objected.

338
00:18:28,440 --> 00:18:32,440
It rose 40 stories straight up as a cliff from the street.

339
00:18:32,540 --> 00:18:37,080
It shaded the buildings to the north for some 22 acres,

340
00:18:37,180 --> 00:18:39,340
and it impaired their real estate values

341
00:18:39,341 --> 00:18:40,800
and their rentability.

342
00:18:40,900 --> 00:18:43,440
So the Equitable Building is often seen as sort of the straw

343
00:18:43,540 --> 00:18:45,480
that broke the camel's back.

344
00:18:45,580 --> 00:18:48,880
Tall skyscrapers had stolen too much fresh air

345
00:18:48,980 --> 00:18:50,080
and sunlight.

346
00:18:50,180 --> 00:18:53,860
In 1916, a zoning ordinance went into effect in New York City

347
00:18:53,960 --> 00:18:57,800
that restricted the size of skyscrapers.

348
00:18:57,900 --> 00:19:01,760
Unlike Chicago, there would be no flat limit on height.

349
00:19:01,860 --> 00:19:05,060
Rather, the ordinance dictated that skyscrapers diminish

350
00:19:05,160 --> 00:19:08,300
in width as they rise in height.

351
00:19:08,301 --> 00:19:11,600
allowing for more air and light at street level.

352
00:19:11,700 --> 00:19:14,100
In essence, what they said was, you may build a building

353
00:19:14,200 --> 00:19:15,540
straight up from the building line,

354
00:19:15,640 --> 00:19:19,140
but then when it meets the imaginary hypotenuse

355
00:19:19,240 --> 00:19:21,440
of the right triangle drawn from across the street,

356
00:19:21,540 --> 00:19:22,940
you have to have a setback.

357
00:19:23,040 --> 00:19:24,140
You can go up some more.

358
00:19:24,240 --> 00:19:25,860
When that hypotenuse hits the building again,

359
00:19:25,960 --> 00:19:27,260
you have to have another setback.

360
00:19:27,360 --> 00:19:29,960
What we got as a result of the setback law

361
00:19:30,060 --> 00:19:32,960
is one building after another that looks like a wedding cake.

362
00:19:33,060 --> 00:19:35,960
And it started to be called wedding cake architecture.

363
00:19:35,961 --> 00:19:38,860
Setback laws did not, however, restrict the height

364
00:19:38,960 --> 00:19:40,840
of the entire building.

365
00:19:40,940 --> 00:19:45,240
On 25% of the lot, a tower could be built as high as technology

366
00:19:45,340 --> 00:19:46,780
would allow.

367
00:19:46,880 --> 00:19:49,680
The bigger the lot size, the bigger the tower could be.

368
00:19:49,780 --> 00:19:52,680
And the biggest towers were yet to come.

369
00:19:56,780 --> 00:19:59,080
It was just a matter of time before someone

370
00:19:59,180 --> 00:20:03,080
would send a skyscraper soaring past the Woolworth Building.

371
00:20:03,081 --> 00:20:05,980
That time was the Roaring Twenties,

372
00:20:06,080 --> 00:20:09,880
and the place was New York City.

373
00:20:09,980 --> 00:20:13,060
Neither John J. Raskob nor Walter P. Chrysler were

374
00:20:13,160 --> 00:20:17,320
architects, but they would be responsible for the two

375
00:20:17,420 --> 00:20:19,960
tallest buildings in the world.

376
00:20:20,060 --> 00:20:23,440
Both men, self-made millionaires who capitalized

377
00:20:23,540 --> 00:20:26,800
on America's growing appetite for automobiles,

378
00:20:26,900 --> 00:20:28,740
had the money to build them.

379
00:20:28,840 --> 00:20:31,340
But it was their enormous egos that

380
00:20:31,341 --> 00:20:37,280
that sent both their skyscrapers into the record books.

381
00:20:37,380 --> 00:20:40,880
John J. Raskob was a financial genius.

382
00:20:40,980 --> 00:20:43,880
As assistant to the treasurer at the DuPont Company,

383
00:20:43,980 --> 00:20:47,860
he gave investment advice on a company in financial turmoil

384
00:20:47,960 --> 00:20:50,220
called General Motors.

385
00:20:50,320 --> 00:20:52,920
Realizing the car industry's potential,

386
00:20:53,020 --> 00:20:57,840
he convinced DuPont to buy GM stock just as the car industry

387
00:20:57,940 --> 00:20:59,260
exploded.

388
00:20:59,261 --> 00:21:04,160
Soon, Raskov was the vice president at GM.

389
00:21:04,260 --> 00:21:06,500
Raskov was not the only self-made man

390
00:21:06,600 --> 00:21:10,100
to join the ranks of the top executives at GM.

391
00:21:10,200 --> 00:21:12,500
Another was Walter Chrysler.

392
00:21:12,600 --> 00:21:15,500
Walter Chrysler was a risk taker,

393
00:21:15,600 --> 00:21:17,820
a brilliant self-taught engineer.

394
00:21:17,920 --> 00:21:21,420
He talked a bank into lending him more than $4,000

395
00:21:21,520 --> 00:21:24,060
to buy a fancy car that had caught his eye.

396
00:21:24,160 --> 00:21:25,620
But he didn't buy the car just because he

397
00:21:25,720 --> 00:21:26,720
liked its good looks.

398
00:21:26,820 --> 00:21:29,260
He had an engineer's fascination with it.

399
00:21:29,261 --> 00:21:31,760
He didn't even drive it until after he had taken it apart

400
00:21:31,860 --> 00:21:33,840
and put it back together a few times.

401
00:21:33,940 --> 00:21:37,200
And he saw, and it may not have been all that hard to see,

402
00:21:37,300 --> 00:21:39,940
that cars were going to be the next big thing.

403
00:21:40,040 --> 00:21:42,040
After his stint at General Motors,

404
00:21:42,140 --> 00:21:45,340
Chrysler invested in two failing car companies.

405
00:21:45,440 --> 00:21:48,320
Within a few years, Chrysler was turning a profit,

406
00:21:48,420 --> 00:21:51,980
selling a new car of his own design and under his own name.

407
00:21:55,880 --> 00:21:59,260
Meanwhile, Raskob became enmeshed in the design

408
00:21:59,261 --> 00:22:01,900
political machinery of New York City.

409
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:05,040
Raskob found himself chairman of the Democratic Party,

410
00:22:05,140 --> 00:22:08,240
backing former governor Al Smith for president

411
00:22:08,340 --> 00:22:12,000
during the 1928 election.

412
00:22:12,100 --> 00:22:15,500
Raskob and Smith became close.

413
00:22:15,600 --> 00:22:17,740
The future Empire State Building would

414
00:22:17,840 --> 00:22:20,780
be the unlikely outcome of their friendship.

415
00:22:23,520 --> 00:22:25,820
Al Smith had several strikes against him.

416
00:22:25,920 --> 00:22:28,460
He was a Roman Catholic at a time when people believed that if

417
00:22:28,461 --> 00:22:29,920
that if we had a Catholic in the White House,

418
00:22:30,020 --> 00:22:32,560
that the pope would be dictating foreign policy.

419
00:22:32,660 --> 00:22:35,700
He had what is arguably the worst New York accent

420
00:22:35,800 --> 00:22:37,000
anybody had ever heard.

421
00:22:37,100 --> 00:22:40,240
And his ability or her ability to give me

422
00:22:40,340 --> 00:22:44,240
the greatest possible aid in devoted service to the people.

423
00:22:44,340 --> 00:22:48,140
And he was an anti-prohibitionist, strike three.

424
00:22:48,240 --> 00:22:52,980
Al Smith lost the election, and now he was out of a job.

425
00:22:53,080 --> 00:22:55,220
The ax fell on Raskob, too.

426
00:22:55,320 --> 00:22:57,920
As General Motors did not think it seemly,

427
00:22:57,921 --> 00:23:00,460
for him to be the chairman of the Democratic Party

428
00:23:00,560 --> 00:23:03,060
and to be working for General Motors at the same time.

429
00:23:03,160 --> 00:23:06,220
Raskob, however, was independently wealthy.

430
00:23:06,320 --> 00:23:07,960
Al Smith wasn't.

431
00:23:08,060 --> 00:23:10,060
Raskob didn't know the first thing about real estate.

432
00:23:10,160 --> 00:23:12,000
The closest thing he had ever come to real estate

433
00:23:12,100 --> 00:23:16,600
was perhaps buying a summer cottage or renting an apartment.

434
00:23:16,700 --> 00:23:21,440
But he thought, I have to do something for my friend Al Smith.

435
00:23:21,540 --> 00:23:26,220
And what he did was decide to build the Empire State Public.

436
00:23:26,221 --> 00:23:29,560
Raskolb came across an available piece of real estate

437
00:23:29,660 --> 00:23:31,020
on Fifth Avenue.

438
00:23:31,120 --> 00:23:33,520
The officers and directors of Empire State,

439
00:23:33,620 --> 00:23:37,860
Incorporated are on the roof of the old Waldorf Astoria

440
00:23:37,960 --> 00:23:42,060
Hotel, about to begin demolition of that ancient and historic

441
00:23:42,160 --> 00:23:42,980
structure.

442
00:23:43,080 --> 00:23:45,680
Smith was basically the front man for the operation.

443
00:23:45,780 --> 00:23:47,980
He would be the spokesman in the general factotum.

444
00:23:48,080 --> 00:23:50,980
He would give people tours when the building was opened.

445
00:23:51,080 --> 00:23:53,980
He would show them the plans before it had opened, et cetera.

446
00:23:53,981 --> 00:23:56,780
While Al Smith was still showing prospective tenants

447
00:23:56,880 --> 00:23:59,460
the plans for the proposed Empire State Building

448
00:23:59,560 --> 00:24:03,360
well before Raskob and Chrysler began their spitting contest,

449
00:24:03,460 --> 00:24:06,460
a third tower was topping out at 40 Wall Street.

450
00:24:06,560 --> 00:24:11,100
In 1929, the Bank of Manhattan Building reached 927 feet,

451
00:24:11,200 --> 00:24:14,600
or 71 stories, snatching the world's tallest title

452
00:24:14,700 --> 00:24:19,500
held by the Woolworth Building for the past 16 years.

453
00:24:19,600 --> 00:24:21,340
But not for long.

454
00:24:21,341 --> 00:24:24,240
The architect for 40 Wall Street had recently split

455
00:24:24,340 --> 00:24:27,020
with his partner, William Van Allen.

456
00:24:27,120 --> 00:24:29,740
When 40 Wall Street topped out, Van Allen was uptown,

457
00:24:29,840 --> 00:24:33,360
working on a building for Mr. Chrysler.

458
00:24:33,460 --> 00:24:35,180
With the success of his automobiles

459
00:24:35,280 --> 00:24:37,860
making him a household name, Chrysler

460
00:24:37,960 --> 00:24:40,620
decided to construct the tallest building in the world,

461
00:24:40,720 --> 00:24:44,000
then name it after himself.

462
00:24:44,100 --> 00:24:47,300
Neither Chrysler nor Van Allen were about to settle

463
00:24:47,400 --> 00:24:50,160
for second tallest.

464
00:24:50,161 --> 00:24:52,060
While Chrysler was on its way up,

465
00:24:52,160 --> 00:24:55,200
Van Allen learned that Severance had gotten a permit

466
00:24:55,300 --> 00:24:57,840
to put a decorative lantern on top of his building,

467
00:24:57,940 --> 00:24:59,200
and for good measure, he was going

468
00:24:59,300 --> 00:25:02,580
to put a 50-foot flagpole on top of that.

469
00:25:02,680 --> 00:25:05,940
Van Allen's response to Severance's hat trick

470
00:25:06,040 --> 00:25:09,280
was a hat trick of his own, a very secretive one.

471
00:25:09,380 --> 00:25:12,220
Van Allen designed a giant spire,

472
00:25:12,320 --> 00:25:15,280
then had it assembled in the fire shaft of the Chrysler

473
00:25:15,380 --> 00:25:17,860
building in order to keep it a secret.

474
00:25:17,861 --> 00:25:20,060
When 40 Wall Street added its lantern

475
00:25:20,160 --> 00:25:22,640
and couldn't possibly go any higher,

476
00:25:22,740 --> 00:25:24,660
Chrysler shot back.

477
00:25:24,760 --> 00:25:27,200
In about 90 minutes, they raised the spire

478
00:25:27,300 --> 00:25:30,540
out of Chrysler's fire shaft and bolted it into place,

479
00:25:30,640 --> 00:25:31,700
and that was it.

480
00:25:31,800 --> 00:25:33,380
Severance was beat.

481
00:25:33,480 --> 00:25:36,840
The Chrysler building was 77 stories tall.

482
00:25:36,940 --> 00:25:41,180
It took nearly 21,000 tons of structural steel,

483
00:25:41,280 --> 00:25:47,860
392,000 rivets, and 200 flights of stairs.

484
00:25:47,861 --> 00:25:50,800
and William Van Allen had won the battle.

485
00:25:50,900 --> 00:25:54,500
It was at this time that John J. Raskob reminded them

486
00:25:54,600 --> 00:25:56,600
they hadn't won the war.

487
00:25:56,700 --> 00:25:59,900
Raskob regarded Chrysler as an upstart, a whippersnapper.

488
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:05,520
When the Chrysler building was topped out at 1,048 feet,

489
00:26:05,620 --> 00:26:08,520
Raskob and Smith said, we are going

490
00:26:08,620 --> 00:26:11,620
to build a building 1,050 feet.

491
00:26:11,720 --> 00:26:16,260
Raskob needed the doors to open on May 1, 1931.

492
00:26:16,261 --> 00:26:19,000
the traditional date to renew leases.

493
00:26:19,100 --> 00:26:21,000
Having given the architects their orders,

494
00:26:21,100 --> 00:26:23,900
Raskov turned his attention to the contractors.

495
00:26:24,000 --> 00:26:27,380
The most famous contractors in the country at the time

496
00:26:27,480 --> 00:26:29,500
were Starrett brothers and Eakin.

497
00:26:29,600 --> 00:26:33,480
They built the Bank of Manhattan building at 40 Wall Street

498
00:26:33,580 --> 00:26:35,140
in record time.

499
00:26:35,240 --> 00:26:41,660
With that on their resume, they made their pitch to Raskov

500
00:26:41,760 --> 00:26:44,020
to build the Empire State Building.

501
00:26:44,021 --> 00:26:46,920
The Sterrett brothers hired the finest iron workers

502
00:26:47,020 --> 00:26:50,200
in the country, men who would defy gravity

503
00:26:50,300 --> 00:26:52,760
to keep construction on schedule.

504
00:26:52,860 --> 00:26:58,140
They began on March 17, 1930, just as the Great Depression

505
00:26:58,240 --> 00:26:59,240
began to set in.

506
00:27:09,180 --> 00:27:11,140
The building rose up from Fifth Avenue

507
00:27:11,141 --> 00:27:14,040
at the rate of more than four floors per week.

508
00:27:18,080 --> 00:27:21,180
Tracks were laid over floors under construction

509
00:27:21,280 --> 00:27:24,380
so cars could deliver supplies to the men.

510
00:27:24,480 --> 00:27:28,260
Virtually all the materials were prefabricated offsite

511
00:27:28,360 --> 00:27:31,700
and delivered to the tower for immediate installation.

512
00:27:31,800 --> 00:27:34,660
The granite exterior curtain wall pieces were pre-cut

513
00:27:34,760 --> 00:27:37,440
at the quarry and then shipped to New York,

514
00:27:37,540 --> 00:27:40,140
where they were fitted into place.

515
00:27:40,141 --> 00:27:42,580
As the Empire State Building neared completion,

516
00:27:42,680 --> 00:27:45,140
it was becoming clear that the architects

517
00:27:45,240 --> 00:27:48,380
had created a masterpiece.

518
00:27:48,480 --> 00:27:51,880
Now, there are setbacks in the tower, but they're minimal.

519
00:27:51,980 --> 00:27:55,820
And what they do is more an aesthetic accomplishment

520
00:27:55,920 --> 00:27:58,820
and a legal, practical one.

521
00:27:58,920 --> 00:28:01,220
It gives the building form, because it rises,

522
00:28:01,320 --> 00:28:03,000
and it very gently tapers.

523
00:28:03,100 --> 00:28:06,860
It's almost as if there's a natural swelling to the building.

524
00:28:06,960 --> 00:28:10,140
As a result, it's a very satisfying form.

525
00:28:10,141 --> 00:28:14,920
Best of all, the building topped out at 1,050 feet,

526
00:28:15,020 --> 00:28:17,980
two feet taller than Chrysler.

527
00:28:18,080 --> 00:28:21,380
But the more Raskob thought about those two feet,

528
00:28:21,480 --> 00:28:24,980
the more uneasy he was.

529
00:28:25,080 --> 00:28:29,820
So what was hatched was arguably the looniest building scheme

530
00:28:29,920 --> 00:28:31,320
since the Tower of Babel.

531
00:28:31,420 --> 00:28:35,340
Raskob and Smith announced with straight, serious faces

532
00:28:35,440 --> 00:28:38,360
that they were going to add a dirigible mooring

533
00:28:38,361 --> 00:28:43,360
to the top of the building, a 200-foot high mooring mast.

534
00:28:43,460 --> 00:28:47,360
And they said that the captain of a dirigible

535
00:28:47,460 --> 00:28:51,400
would drop a line over from his craft,

536
00:28:51,500 --> 00:28:56,120
and from the gondola of the dirigible,

537
00:28:56,220 --> 00:29:00,720
a gangplank would be dropped, and people would happily walk

538
00:29:00,820 --> 00:29:05,360
1,250 feet up in the air with nothing down between them

539
00:29:05,460 --> 00:29:06,360
and the street below.

540
00:29:06,361 --> 00:29:09,000
Although half-hearted attempts were made,

541
00:29:09,100 --> 00:29:12,460
dirigibles did not dock atop the Empire State Building.

542
00:29:12,560 --> 00:29:14,760
The idea quietly died.

543
00:29:14,860 --> 00:29:18,240
What the mooring mass did do was push the Empire State Building

544
00:29:18,340 --> 00:29:23,740
to 1,250 feet, 200 feet higher than Chrysler.

545
00:29:23,840 --> 00:29:26,840
It was the biggest, craziest hat trick of them all.

546
00:29:26,940 --> 00:29:29,020
Raskop had just won the war.

547
00:29:29,120 --> 00:29:31,060
When my sister and I were children,

548
00:29:31,160 --> 00:29:33,860
we used to debate whether Chrysler or the Empire State

549
00:29:33,960 --> 00:29:35,180
Building were better buildings.

550
00:29:35,181 --> 00:29:38,520
My attitude was, Empire is bigger, so it was better.

551
00:29:38,620 --> 00:29:42,480
I was just into the Empire State Building because it was big,

552
00:29:42,580 --> 00:29:45,420
like a lot of boys, I suppose.

553
00:29:45,520 --> 00:29:48,420
The 102-story Empire State Building

554
00:29:48,520 --> 00:29:51,900
was hailed as a marvelous success when it opened on time

555
00:29:52,000 --> 00:29:56,000
and under budget on May 1, 1931.

556
00:29:56,100 --> 00:29:58,400
But the confidence that it allowed New York City

557
00:29:58,500 --> 00:30:00,880
to build the tallest skyscrapers in the world

558
00:30:00,980 --> 00:30:02,940
was about to disappear.

559
00:30:02,941 --> 00:30:07,940
economic collapse and a world war would end the race for height.

560
00:30:11,940 --> 00:30:13,980
The Empire State and Chrysler buildings

561
00:30:14,080 --> 00:30:17,980
were conceived in the heady days of the Roaring Twenties.

562
00:30:18,080 --> 00:30:23,000
Unfortunately, they opened during the Depression of the 1930s.

563
00:30:23,100 --> 00:30:26,060
New York no longer needed all the office space they offered.

564
00:30:26,160 --> 00:30:29,360
A building that opened at 50% occupancy

565
00:30:29,460 --> 00:30:31,240
was considered a borderline case

566
00:30:31,241 --> 00:30:34,940
case in terms of making it go financially.

567
00:30:35,040 --> 00:30:39,820
The Empire State Building opened with a 23% occupancy,

568
00:30:39,920 --> 00:30:42,320
which practically doomed it to failure.

569
00:30:42,420 --> 00:30:44,220
Within months of opening, the building

570
00:30:44,320 --> 00:30:47,020
was nicknamed the empty state building.

571
00:30:47,120 --> 00:30:49,960
The only reliable income the skyscraper generated

572
00:30:50,060 --> 00:30:52,460
was from its observatory.

573
00:30:52,560 --> 00:30:55,360
The nickels and dimes of people wanting to view New York

574
00:30:55,460 --> 00:31:01,240
from the clouds paid off 2% of the building's total cost.

575
00:31:01,241 --> 00:31:03,440
World War II followed the Depression

576
00:31:03,540 --> 00:31:05,640
as the Empire State and Chrysler buildings

577
00:31:05,740 --> 00:31:08,880
came to symbolize a bygone era.

578
00:31:08,980 --> 00:31:13,480
As if to emphasize the point, a B-25 bomber lost in a thick fog

579
00:31:13,580 --> 00:31:17,260
crashed into the Empire State Building in 1945.

580
00:31:17,360 --> 00:31:19,400
All three passengers aboard the plane,

581
00:31:19,500 --> 00:31:22,100
plus 11 people in the building, were killed.

582
00:31:27,440 --> 00:31:30,360
Technological changes after World War II,

583
00:31:30,361 --> 00:31:33,400
such as fluorescent lights and air conditioning

584
00:31:33,500 --> 00:31:37,940
would impact the look of the modern skyscraper.

585
00:31:38,040 --> 00:31:40,800
Every office no longer had to be close to a window

586
00:31:40,900 --> 00:31:41,840
for light and air.

587
00:31:44,500 --> 00:31:46,940
Technology also allowed architects

588
00:31:47,040 --> 00:31:50,040
to sheath skyscrapers in new materials.

589
00:31:50,140 --> 00:31:52,620
In the 1950s, the Lever Brothers Soap Company

590
00:31:52,720 --> 00:31:56,260
commissioned an office complex on Park Avenue in New York

591
00:31:56,360 --> 00:32:00,360
that would project an image of cleanliness and purity.

592
00:32:00,361 --> 00:32:03,160
Packaged in cool green glass, Leverhaus

593
00:32:03,260 --> 00:32:06,840
became the first glass curtain skyscraper.

594
00:32:06,940 --> 00:32:08,840
Across the street, the Seagram Company

595
00:32:08,940 --> 00:32:13,040
would construct an elegantly simple black box of a skyscraper

596
00:32:13,140 --> 00:32:15,080
with a street level plaza.

597
00:32:15,180 --> 00:32:18,580
Steel and glass and the materials of the modern world

598
00:32:18,680 --> 00:32:22,880
are very much celebrated and embraced for the construction

599
00:32:22,980 --> 00:32:25,880
of the image of the new modern skyscrapers.

600
00:32:25,980 --> 00:32:29,360
And so the buildings of steel bones

601
00:32:29,361 --> 00:32:32,400
and glass skin become very much the aesthetic

602
00:32:32,500 --> 00:32:33,940
of the modern movement.

603
00:32:34,040 --> 00:32:37,640
Office space in New York was a valuable commodity again.

604
00:32:37,740 --> 00:32:41,300
Even the old Empire State Building was finally in the black.

605
00:32:41,400 --> 00:32:45,080
Glass and steel skyscrapers soon began popping up everywhere

606
00:32:45,180 --> 00:32:47,440
to meet the growing demand.

607
00:32:47,540 --> 00:32:50,540
Amidst this landscape of steel and glass high rise

608
00:32:50,640 --> 00:32:55,120
construction, two cities reignited an old rivalry as each

609
00:32:55,220 --> 00:32:59,360
drove to surpass a 40-year record.

610
00:32:59,361 --> 00:33:03,360
In the late 1960s, the Port Authority of New York and New

611
00:33:03,460 --> 00:33:07,360
Jersey decided to construct its headquarters on land

612
00:33:07,460 --> 00:33:10,340
located at the end of Manhattan Island.

613
00:33:10,440 --> 00:33:12,880
But these wouldn't be just any headquarters.

614
00:33:15,440 --> 00:33:17,740
Ego was a very difficult thing to keep down.

615
00:33:17,840 --> 00:33:20,940
It was an ego trip for the Port Authority of New York

616
00:33:21,040 --> 00:33:25,420
and New Jersey to build not just one building that was the world

617
00:33:25,520 --> 00:33:26,980
tallest, but two.

618
00:33:26,981 --> 00:33:28,980
The Empire State Building had been

619
00:33:29,080 --> 00:33:32,920
the tallest in the world since 1931.

620
00:33:33,020 --> 00:33:36,560
But the Port Authority was about to change all that.

621
00:33:36,660 --> 00:33:39,100
Further, these two skyscrapers would be constructed

622
00:33:39,200 --> 00:33:40,960
using a system that had been abandoned

623
00:33:41,060 --> 00:33:43,560
when skyscrapers were invented.

624
00:33:43,660 --> 00:33:46,100
The outer walls of the World Trade Center towers

625
00:33:46,200 --> 00:33:48,000
would be load-bearing walls.

626
00:33:48,100 --> 00:33:51,040
The structural steel system of the towers

627
00:33:51,140 --> 00:33:56,980
is unique and not conventional in that the city

628
00:33:56,981 --> 00:34:00,520
The exterior walls carry vertical load.

629
00:34:00,620 --> 00:34:03,680
A great dividend that was crude was

630
00:34:03,780 --> 00:34:06,860
that there was no interior columns

631
00:34:06,960 --> 00:34:09,720
until one got to the elevator shafts

632
00:34:09,820 --> 00:34:11,460
in the center of the building.

633
00:34:11,560 --> 00:34:14,060
This allowed for the maximum rentable space

634
00:34:14,160 --> 00:34:15,860
inside the towers.

635
00:34:15,960 --> 00:34:19,400
Before the first tower even topped out at 110 stories

636
00:34:19,500 --> 00:34:24,480
or 1,368 feet, there were tenants occupying the lower

637
00:34:24,580 --> 00:34:25,480
floors.

638
00:34:25,481 --> 00:34:31,660
Obviously, we were pleased to be working on a new world's

639
00:34:31,760 --> 00:34:33,660
tallest skyscraper at the time.

640
00:34:33,760 --> 00:34:35,720
But only for a short time.

641
00:34:35,820 --> 00:34:38,900
Retailers Sears and Roebuck decided to consolidate

642
00:34:39,000 --> 00:34:41,760
its corporate offices into one building,

643
00:34:41,860 --> 00:34:43,740
smack in the middle of the city that once

644
00:34:43,840 --> 00:34:46,000
been New York's rival.

645
00:34:46,100 --> 00:34:48,060
Having rescinded its restrictive height

646
00:34:48,160 --> 00:34:52,280
regulation some years before, Chicago was ready to rise.

647
00:34:52,281 --> 00:34:57,280
The architects drafted an innovative plan that clustered

648
00:34:57,380 --> 00:35:01,260
nine tube-like skyscrapers together on the same site.

649
00:35:01,360 --> 00:35:04,920
What was unique about Sears was this bundle tube concept.

650
00:35:05,020 --> 00:35:08,300
One of our architectural design partners was a smoker,

651
00:35:08,400 --> 00:35:12,340
and so they got out of his pack of cigarettes

652
00:35:12,440 --> 00:35:14,600
and actually took those cigarettes and said,

653
00:35:14,700 --> 00:35:16,980
here, this is what the structure is going to look like.

654
00:35:17,080 --> 00:35:19,300
Approximately halfway up the building,

655
00:35:19,301 --> 00:35:23,540
two of the nine tubular mini skyscrapers top out.

656
00:35:23,640 --> 00:35:26,780
The rest continue upward another 16 stories,

657
00:35:26,880 --> 00:35:30,040
when two more tubes top out.

658
00:35:30,140 --> 00:35:34,340
Three of the remaining tubes end another 24 stories higher,

659
00:35:34,440 --> 00:35:36,960
leaving the last two tubes to climax

660
00:35:37,060 --> 00:35:39,920
in the topmost portion of the building.

661
00:35:40,020 --> 00:35:43,420
This reinforced tube design would make the Sears Tower

662
00:35:43,520 --> 00:35:47,420
exceptionally strong, able to withstand the great forces

663
00:35:47,421 --> 00:35:52,420
against giant projects of staggering heights.

664
00:35:52,520 --> 00:35:56,420
Sears' design was the most cost-effective and efficient.

665
00:35:56,520 --> 00:35:59,160
But when the tapered design was factored into Sears'

666
00:35:59,260 --> 00:36:04,640
required floor space, something dawned on the architects.

667
00:36:04,740 --> 00:36:06,700
All of a sudden, it became very evident

668
00:36:06,800 --> 00:36:09,480
that this building was going to become very tall just because

669
00:36:09,580 --> 00:36:12,840
of its size and the functional requirements.

670
00:36:12,940 --> 00:36:15,940
So it was really an evolutionary thing.

671
00:36:15,941 --> 00:36:18,740
And at that point then, when everybody began to consider,

672
00:36:18,840 --> 00:36:20,340
well, let's go a little bit taller

673
00:36:20,440 --> 00:36:22,680
and let's make it the tallest building in the world.

674
00:36:22,780 --> 00:36:24,680
Now it was time to build it.

675
00:36:24,780 --> 00:36:28,020
The first step was hiring the right people for the job.

676
00:36:28,120 --> 00:36:32,260
The guy that was in charge of the construction foreseers,

677
00:36:32,360 --> 00:36:34,120
he was interviewing everybody.

678
00:36:34,220 --> 00:36:36,660
And when they got to me, he said, well,

679
00:36:36,760 --> 00:36:39,600
how many 110-storey buildings have you built?

680
00:36:39,700 --> 00:36:41,000
I was glib that day.

681
00:36:41,100 --> 00:36:44,640
And I said, I never built 110, but I built 40, 50,

682
00:36:44,641 --> 00:36:46,380
and 220s.

683
00:36:46,480 --> 00:36:50,280
Amazingly, Sears managed to come in on time and on budget.

684
00:36:50,380 --> 00:36:52,580
When you just have that little thing in the back of your mind,

685
00:36:52,680 --> 00:36:54,780
I'm going to bring this building to this site by truck,

686
00:36:54,880 --> 00:36:58,360
it's a very important thing to have your job laid out right

687
00:36:58,460 --> 00:37:00,720
in the first place, because you've

688
00:37:00,820 --> 00:37:04,020
got to move the material in there.

689
00:37:04,120 --> 00:37:09,940
By September 10, 1974, 1,454 feet of vertical glass

690
00:37:10,040 --> 00:37:13,200
and steel had come together on two blocks

691
00:37:13,201 --> 00:37:15,200
in the middle of Chicago.

692
00:37:15,300 --> 00:37:19,700
One and a half million tourists visit the Sears Tower every year,

693
00:37:19,800 --> 00:37:23,140
most traveling to the 100th floor observatory

694
00:37:23,240 --> 00:37:25,140
for the spectacular view.

695
00:37:25,240 --> 00:37:28,440
Ada Louise Huxtable once referred to it as 110 stories

696
00:37:28,540 --> 00:37:30,560
of soaring nonchalance.

697
00:37:30,660 --> 00:37:34,420
And I love that quote because I think that it shows that you

698
00:37:34,520 --> 00:37:38,060
don't have to have a skyscraper with a hat on top

699
00:37:38,160 --> 00:37:43,200
for the building to really etch a profile against the sky.

700
00:37:43,201 --> 00:37:48,780
The 110-story Sears Tower is 86 feet taller than the World Trade Center.

701
00:37:48,920 --> 00:37:52,000
Although it steps back, I must say,

702
00:37:52,220 --> 00:37:55,800
but in height, it is the world's tallest.

703
00:37:59,600 --> 00:38:04,080
In 1993, the World Trade Center was robbed by an explosion

704
00:38:04,081 --> 00:38:07,460
planted by terrorists in the garage of the North Tower.

705
00:38:08,400 --> 00:38:12,400
A 1,200-pound bomb destroyed a five-story building

706
00:38:12,401 --> 00:38:13,560
at the Trade Center's base.

707
00:38:16,400 --> 00:38:20,200
But the towers were pretty much unscathed,

708
00:38:20,300 --> 00:38:23,040
but the structure of the tower itself

709
00:38:23,140 --> 00:38:25,380
was not affected at all.

710
00:38:25,480 --> 00:38:28,280
Skyscrapers like the World Trade Center and Sears

711
00:38:28,380 --> 00:38:31,680
have to be capable of withstanding cataclysmic events

712
00:38:31,780 --> 00:38:33,980
without serious damage.

713
00:38:34,080 --> 00:38:36,600
Redundancy is built into most of these structures,

714
00:38:36,700 --> 00:38:38,860
which means that no single beam or rivet

715
00:38:38,960 --> 00:38:41,160
takes all the burden of the building.

716
00:38:41,161 --> 00:38:44,200
You would have to remove a lot of the structure

717
00:38:44,300 --> 00:38:47,560
before it would be incapable of sharing the load.

718
00:38:47,660 --> 00:38:50,500
And so they are very tough structures.

719
00:38:50,600 --> 00:38:55,580
In fact, skyscrapers are symbols of strength and stability.

720
00:38:55,680 --> 00:38:58,840
Both of these cities really show that the skyscraper

721
00:38:58,940 --> 00:39:02,280
is kind of our chart, our Notre Dame.

722
00:39:02,380 --> 00:39:06,920
It exemplifies American ambition, the triumph of capitalism,

723
00:39:07,020 --> 00:39:10,220
and most of all, how architects have transformed technology

724
00:39:10,221 --> 00:39:10,921
into art.

725
00:39:16,720 --> 00:39:19,160
No building threatened the Sears Tower's title

726
00:39:19,260 --> 00:39:23,640
as world's tallest building until an economic boom in the 1980s

727
00:39:23,740 --> 00:39:26,700
swept through Asia.

728
00:39:26,800 --> 00:39:30,400
When a small town or a country on the make

729
00:39:30,500 --> 00:39:34,240
wants to prove that it's arrived on the world stage,

730
00:39:34,340 --> 00:39:35,280
what do they do?

731
00:39:35,380 --> 00:39:36,680
They build a skyscraper.

732
00:39:36,780 --> 00:39:38,740
American architect Caesar Pelley

733
00:39:38,741 --> 00:39:42,340
designed the Petronas Towers in Kuala Lumpur.

734
00:39:42,440 --> 00:39:48,080
The building topped out in 1998 at 1,476 feet.

735
00:39:48,180 --> 00:39:50,980
For the first time in the history of skyscrapers,

736
00:39:51,080 --> 00:39:54,980
the title was held by a building outside the United States.

737
00:39:55,080 --> 00:39:56,860
Why would you build the tallest building in the world

738
00:39:56,960 --> 00:39:58,520
in Southeast Asia?

739
00:39:58,620 --> 00:39:59,620
Pride.

740
00:39:59,720 --> 00:40:01,100
You're going to say, we're going to have the tallest building

741
00:40:01,200 --> 00:40:02,300
in the world.

742
00:40:02,400 --> 00:40:05,000
It has nothing to do with money, nothing to do with art.

743
00:40:05,100 --> 00:40:07,100
The 88-story Petronas Towers

744
00:40:07,101 --> 00:40:11,100
are taller than the Sears Tower by 22 feet, or are they?

745
00:40:11,200 --> 00:40:13,340
When you're standing on the top story at Sears,

746
00:40:13,440 --> 00:40:16,680
you're looking down over 20 floors to the highest person

747
00:40:16,780 --> 00:40:19,380
that can be on the Patronus Towers.

748
00:40:19,480 --> 00:40:23,180
Chicago protested the loss of title

749
00:40:23,280 --> 00:40:24,920
for having the world's tallest building.

750
00:40:25,020 --> 00:40:27,660
And one of the organizations that

751
00:40:27,760 --> 00:40:29,420
worries about these things, the Council

752
00:40:29,520 --> 00:40:31,300
on Tall Buildings and Urban Habitat,

753
00:40:31,400 --> 00:40:34,520
developed these separate definitions.

754
00:40:34,620 --> 00:40:36,100
The council determines height

755
00:40:36,101 --> 00:40:38,840
by measuring a line from the sidewalk of the main entrance

756
00:40:38,940 --> 00:40:41,400
to the structural top of the building.

757
00:40:41,500 --> 00:40:44,600
The council includes spires in its definition of structural

758
00:40:44,700 --> 00:40:48,080
top, but not TV antennas or flagpoles.

759
00:40:48,180 --> 00:40:50,340
By this definition, the Petronus Towers

760
00:40:50,440 --> 00:40:52,180
are taller than Sears.

761
00:40:52,280 --> 00:40:55,460
But the council also mentions three other criteria

762
00:40:55,560 --> 00:40:57,260
for determining height.

763
00:40:57,360 --> 00:41:00,120
By the definition of highest occupied floor,

764
00:41:00,220 --> 00:41:03,720
Sears beats Petronus Towers by 202 feet.

765
00:41:03,721 --> 00:41:06,560
And by definition of height to the top of the roof,

766
00:41:06,660 --> 00:41:11,100
Sears also beats Petronus Towers by some 208 feet.

767
00:41:11,200 --> 00:41:14,400
But by definition of height to the top of spire or antenna,

768
00:41:14,500 --> 00:41:18,500
the World Trade Center beats them both, Sears by 21 feet

769
00:41:18,600 --> 00:41:22,340
and Petronus Towers by 245 feet.

770
00:41:22,440 --> 00:41:24,400
Obviously, I'm prejudiced on this subject,

771
00:41:24,500 --> 00:41:29,120
but I feel that the true measurement, what makes a building

772
00:41:29,220 --> 00:41:31,480
different than just a tower, is people.

773
00:41:31,580 --> 00:41:33,720
What is the height that people go to?

774
00:41:33,721 --> 00:41:37,760
Talk of a new world's tallest will always grab headlines

775
00:41:37,860 --> 00:41:39,460
on the front pages.

776
00:41:39,560 --> 00:41:43,160
The question is, how high can skyscrapers go?

777
00:41:43,260 --> 00:41:47,260
Well, in 1956, Frank Lloyd Wright proposed

778
00:41:47,360 --> 00:41:53,380
an absolutely outlandish, mile high Illinois skyscraper

779
00:41:53,480 --> 00:41:54,580
for Chicago.

780
00:41:54,680 --> 00:41:57,780
It was supposed to have 528 stories.

781
00:41:57,880 --> 00:42:00,980
It was supposed to have an occupancy of a high-high

782
00:42:00,981 --> 00:42:05,660
It was supposed to have an occupancy of 100,000 people.

783
00:42:05,760 --> 00:42:07,420
I mean, here's Frank Lloyd Wright.

784
00:42:07,520 --> 00:42:11,360
He has a 26-foot-high drawing of the thing.

785
00:42:11,460 --> 00:42:13,000
It's a great publicity stunt.

786
00:42:13,100 --> 00:42:17,360
Wright was known for being an opponent of cities.

787
00:42:17,460 --> 00:42:19,240
He said everyone should live on a farm.

788
00:42:19,340 --> 00:42:22,940
And here he is proposing an enormously dense skyscraper

789
00:42:23,040 --> 00:42:24,600
right in the middle of Chicago.

790
00:42:24,700 --> 00:42:25,900
So go figure.

791
00:42:26,000 --> 00:42:30,980
Maybe Wright wasn't so far off the mark with his mile high.

792
00:42:30,981 --> 00:42:33,320
I think we can go much, much taller

793
00:42:33,420 --> 00:42:35,220
than we have built in the past.

794
00:42:35,320 --> 00:42:37,960
We looked at a building about 10, 15 years ago

795
00:42:38,060 --> 00:42:39,860
that was twice the height of Sears.

796
00:42:39,960 --> 00:42:42,600
Engineers can build it, but they may not

797
00:42:42,700 --> 00:42:46,960
be able to get people inside it.

798
00:42:47,060 --> 00:42:49,140
What begins to come into play, actually,

799
00:42:49,240 --> 00:42:52,140
are other issues other than structural engineering that

800
00:42:52,240 --> 00:42:54,240
are actually more difficult to solve.

801
00:42:54,340 --> 00:42:57,640
It's almost exponential, the difficulty that you get into.

802
00:42:57,641 --> 00:43:00,280
Just the whole issue of the number of people

803
00:43:00,380 --> 00:43:04,040
that you're concentrating in a particular area.

804
00:43:04,140 --> 00:43:07,280
Each day, roughly 50,000 workers commute

805
00:43:07,380 --> 00:43:10,220
to the World Trade Center, the population

806
00:43:10,320 --> 00:43:12,680
of an average American town squeezed

807
00:43:12,780 --> 00:43:15,800
into the area of two city blocks.

808
00:43:15,900 --> 00:43:17,760
Buildings taller than the Sears Tower

809
00:43:17,860 --> 00:43:20,160
will have even more people to deal with.

810
00:43:20,260 --> 00:43:23,640
The real obstacle is the transportation

811
00:43:23,740 --> 00:43:25,060
to the upper stories.

812
00:43:25,160 --> 00:43:27,640
So elevators is really one of the principal concerns

813
00:43:27,641 --> 00:43:30,680
for very tall buildings, just as at the beginning of the century

814
00:43:30,780 --> 00:43:33,920
it became the vehicle by which tall buildings became popular.

815
00:43:34,020 --> 00:43:35,680
None of this will prevent the construction

816
00:43:35,780 --> 00:43:38,120
of another world's tallest building.

817
00:43:38,220 --> 00:43:39,660
Gigantism is wonderful.

818
00:43:39,760 --> 00:43:41,680
It's great for barroom arguments,

819
00:43:41,780 --> 00:43:44,560
and my city is better than your city, and all about that.

820
00:43:44,660 --> 00:43:46,860
But ultimately, we have to think about these buildings

821
00:43:46,960 --> 00:43:49,800
in terms of their impact on the human environment.

822
00:43:49,900 --> 00:43:51,860
Skyscrapers are one of America's

823
00:43:51,960 --> 00:43:54,700
unique cultural contributions.

824
00:43:54,800 --> 00:43:56,600
They may be built all over the world,

825
00:43:56,601 --> 00:43:59,240
but they were created in America

826
00:43:59,340 --> 00:44:03,140
and reached their greatest heights in America.

827
00:44:03,240 --> 00:44:06,340
People around the world recognize the skyscraper

828
00:44:06,440 --> 00:44:10,280
as an image of America, as a symbol of America.

829
00:44:10,380 --> 00:44:13,320
The skyscraper is certainly the most spectacular expression

830
00:44:13,420 --> 00:44:18,760
of American know-how and aspiration and optimism.

831
00:44:18,860 --> 00:44:22,300
Why work on it if it's not going to be one of the greats?

832
00:44:22,400 --> 00:44:24,220
I'd love to do a hard one.

833
00:44:24,320 --> 00:44:26,600
Why not?

